lines += ['', '## Pass 131: coming verbs', '', 'Saved 67 simple coming responses under āpayati 1200, āgata 1045 and āviśati 1437, distinguishing present stems, old past stems and the eastern āisā family. Regional contractions and s/h realizations are qualified in the decisions. Eleven Pothwari aś/as/ac responses are held because CDIAL 227 and 1044 explicitly discuss competing atyeti/āgacchati derivations. Western aviyu/auiyu/aviyo past forms remain for analogical or productive present-stem morphology rather than being assigned mechanically to āgata; paired present/past responses also need separate analysis.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 132: descending verbs', '', 'Saved Kathoriya gir and Kochila Tharu girale under *girati 4159 and Dang uṭər under uttarati 1770, preserving the directional gloss and retroflex notation. Sixteen simple jhar-/jar- descending responses are held: 5328 *jhaṭati explicitly allows 5346 *jharati for Nepali jharnu, so the comparison does not select a unique root. Negative/auxiliary responses, short uṭ, and other unrelated descending stems remain for separate work.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 133: drinking and consuming', '', 'Saved 32 simple drinking responses under pibati 8209, khādati 3865 and the khādita past branch 3865-2. Fresh Platts page 868 explicitly supports drink as a meaning of khānā; the full public response and URL are preserved in pass133-platts-research.json. Regional deaspiration and l-endings are qualified. Water-plus-verb compounds, light-verb forms, the pid-/piḍ- series, and duk-/kam- responses remain for separate work; no claim is made that every drink response comes from pibati.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 134: eating verbs', '', 'Saved 47 simple eating responses, separating khādati 3865 from khādita 3865-2 and its explicitly documented analogical khādhũ/khādhā past forms. Dental/retroflex transcription and western l-extensions are qualified in the decisions. Hadothi jeo is linked to jemati 5267, and Marwari jīmṇa to *jimyati/*jimmati 5267-3; the primary entry records a disputed deeper Munda connection, which these links do not settle. Compound eating responses and the short jim- branch remain for separate work.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 135: Dardic eating stems', '', 'Saved ten Khowar, Kalasha, Gawri and Dameli eating responses under 10507 yuvate, citing the explicit eat sense in subsection 2. The canonical node has no separate subsection-2 node and displays yokes, so the full primary prose is essential. Turner tentatively derives Khowar žib- from *yuvvati with expressive doubling; that qualification, verbal endings and source spacing are retained in every decision. The distinct ravate grain-eating family 10645 was compared.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 136: tongue variants', '', 'Saved 24 tongue responses under jihvā 5228, with eastern affrication, short jī/ji and h/glide variants qualified in the decisions. Nine records are held: Bishnupriya ju needs distinction between jihvā and juhū, while jibi/jiβi needs an inflection-versus-*jihviya decision. Full 5231 includes Prakrit tongue, not merely the modern tongue-scraper sense. Persian-family forms, Khowar lixinī/liginī, compounds and source-gap j b remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 137: short nose forms', '', 'Saved 18 ṇak/ṇake and nako/nakho responses under *nakka 6909. Initial retroflex notation is retained without claiming direct Prakrit retention; aspiration and final vowels are qualified. The full nasta 7031 entry was read, but nathuna/nethuna nose cannot be assigned solely from nose-ring or nostril comparisons. Longer regional formations and the Khowar naskar series remain for independent analysis.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 138: ear extensions', '', 'Saved seven ear responses under karṇa 2830, including qualified kānḍ- comparisons to explicit Prakrit kaṇṇaḍaya and Pashai kaṇ(ḍ)-. Nineteen kānṭ-/kaṇṭ-/kānṭh- responses are held because those sources do not establish their voiceless retroflex extension. Karṇaka 2831 was also inspected and does not supply that suffix. Simple and learned forms retain their original spelling, and no survey transcription was corrected.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 139: residual neck/throat terms', '', 'Saved two Bengali gola/gɔla neck records under gala 4070, retaining vowel notation. Two Bishnupriya nar neck records are held pending a regional attestation to distinguish the nāḍī/naḍa comparisons and support the neck sense. Ushoji tūrūṛ throat remains without a supported match after homepage discovery. These five records exhaust the then-unresearched exact neck/throat glosses, but held cases remain unresolved and this is not completion of the joint survey project.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 140: hand and lower-limb comparisons', '', 'Saved Maiya hā / hatʰ hand under hasta 14024, whose full entry explicitly supplies hā and hātha. Saved five Bishnupriya/Kullui jaŋ/dʒəŋ foot responses under jaṅghā 5082, flagging the foot/leg semantic range and preserving the survey gloss. Hajong theŋ and pata compounds, Bengali learned pod and Kullui residual forms remain for independent research. The language ID kul was verified as Kullui in the canonical language table.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 141: different/separate variants', '', 'Saved 15 simple and repeated alag/olag/alga different responses under alagna 700, whose full entry explicitly documents alag/alga across eastern and western Indo-Aryan. Vowel variation, shortened repeated elements and distributive repetition are qualified. Extra-suffix forms, agal- transpositions, mixed alagalag/phorok, and Hajong alda/alada remain for separate work.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 142: separate/different families', '', 'Saved 24 bhin-/bin-, nyar- and begle responses under bhinna 9498, *anyākāra 404 and the extended *viyagnala node 11826-2. Full competing entries *nirālaya 7277 and *viyagra 11827 were compared: the latter concerns the foolish sense of beglo, while separate/different is explicit under 11826. Repetition, vowel endings and deaspiration are qualified. Mixed rokom/rakam responses, extra d or other unexplained extensions remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 143: other and Persian separate', '', 'Saved seven dosar/dūsar different responses under *dviḥsara 6676, using the explicit another/second senses and qualifying distributive repetition. Eight judo/juḍo-type responses are held: fresh Platts page 378 establishes Persian judā separate/different, but discovery found no suitable lexical Persian donor node. A Kalasha word pointing to a generic Persian category is not a replacement donor; CDIAL 5090 cold is a false match. Full Platts response and URL are preserved in pass143-platts-research.json.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 144: like/same and one-plus-like', '', 'Saved 35 same records with 54 assignment rows: 16 simple sarka/harka forms under sadṛkṣa 13119 and 19 clear ek + sarka/harka responses with ordered *ekka 2462-2 and sadṛkṣa components. Both lexical parts are represented, retaining spacing, s/h and aspiration variation and inflection. This is a compositional analysis of survey responses, not a newly reconstructed ancient compound. Short aksār/iksār, extra g in ekhārigo and other same expressions remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 145: one-plus-like expressions', '', 'Saved 20 same responses with 38 assignment rows: two simple jasa/jeso forms under yādṛśa 10458, and 18 ek + jaisa-type expressions with ordered *ekka 2462-2 and yādṛśa components. The full primary entry explicitly supplies jaisā/jaso/jasā. Regional vowels, affrication and śy notation are retained and qualified, and original spacing is unchanged.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 146: short one-plus-like expressions', '', 'Saved 26 ek sā/ekso-type same responses with 52 ordered component links to *ekka 2462-2 and sadṛśa 13120. Platts page 621 explicitly distinguishes comparative sā from the separate intensifying suffix sā; the comparative entry documents a suffixed sadṛśa derivation. These links identify that documented lexical base, not a direct unsuffixed sound change. Full primary responses are preserved, source spacing and inflection retained, and aksār/iksār and longer formations remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 147: further comparative variants', '', 'Saved nine same responses with 15 assignment rows: six ek + jīsā/jaīso/jeso-type compounds under *ekka 2462-2 and yādṛśa 10458, and three Bhilali/Vasavi hərkhā/ṣarko/ṣarkhẽ forms under sadṛkṣa 13119. Full primary prose includes Old Gujarati jisiuṁ and western sarkhũ/sarkho comparanda. Vowel variation, ś/ź/ṣ notation, aspiration and local transmission are explicitly qualified; source spellings remain unchanged.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 148: morning families', '', 'Saved 54 morning records: 35 sakal/sakar variants under sakāla 13067, eight contracted ben/byan responses under *vibhāna 11813, and 11 bhinsar/bhunsar responses under *vibhāniḥsāra 11813a. Fresh Platts page 663 explicitly equates sakār with sa-kāl, and page 193 supplies bhinusār beside bhinsār. The alternative MIA vibhāyana history of the shorter dawn family and regional vowel, aspiration and liquid variation remain qualified. Full public responses and cross-reference follow-up are preserved. Compound morning responses and unexplained extra suffixes remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 149: near postpositions', '', 'Saved 45 near responses under lagna 10893. The full entry explicitly supplies Gujarati lāgu and Marathi lāgī̃ near, documents oblique postpositional uses, and its addendum supplies Old Punjabi lavai and Punjabi/Hindi lave. Gemination, aspiration, vowels and local transmission are qualified. The separate present verb lagyati 10895 was compared. Extra ṭ/th formations, shortened lhe and mixed or multiword responses remain for independent analysis.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 150: wet stem families', '', 'Saved 56 wet responses: 20 under *bhiyagna 9500, 27 under *bhiyajyate 9502, five under *tinta 5812 and four under nasal *bhiyañjati 9503. Full articles document the bhig/bhij distinction and eastern l-participles. Twelve sin/sinn/sun responses are held because snih 13798 and svinna 13934 explicitly offer competing histories. Regional consonant notation, aspiration, endings and transmission remain qualified. Light-verb compounds, extra elements and pig- forms remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 151: wetting and competing sijj roots', '', 'Saved seven Dotyali/Jaunsari ruji/rujeko wet responses under rudhyate 10779, whose addendum explicitly gives Nepali rujhanu get wet and rejects the rīyate alternative. Thirteen Pothwari sijya/sija responses are held: sicyate 13393 and svidyati 13933 both document western sijj wet and cross-reference the competing derivation. Sikta 13388 was also inspected. Sound notation and participial endings are qualified in the accepted evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 152: many and abundance', '', 'Saved 18 many responses under bahutva 9190, ghana 4424, adhika 250 and aneka 345. Full bahutva explicitly includes the bahuterā/batherā extension and notes prabhūta influence. Fresh Platts page 35 confirms abundant/more numerous for adhik. Regional vowels, aspiration, endings and learned or cross-IA transmission remain qualified. Extra suffixes and mixed responses remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 153: dher quantitative forms', '', 'Saved seven dental dher/dherai many responses under the specific *dhera subsection 5599-2, retaining vowel and aspiration variation. Gojri ḍʰeṛ is held between *ḍhera 5599 and the explicit ḍheṛ heap reflex of *ḍheḍḍha 5598. Mukta 10151 was also read after discovery but does not by itself establish the residual muc/mukṭa many forms; those remain unresearched rather than forced into its released sense.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 154: bad adjective families', '', 'Saved 16 bad responses: eight western bhunḍ forms under *bhuṇḍa 9530-7, five mando/manda/mandī forms under manda 9754-5, and three Kullui buɽ forms under *bura 9289. Full primary prose distinguishes the relevant defective, belly and pig families. Four Gojri/Kaithal bhunḍa responses are held between Punjabi *bhuṇṭa 9530-5 and Hindi/Gujarati *bhuṇḍa 9530-7; surface ḍ alone does not decide that reconstructed subsection. Negative phrases, extra suffixes and mixed alternatives remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 155: ash families', '', 'Saved 49 ash responses: 19 char/car/sar forms under kṣāra 3674, 27 western Bhil extended rukhəḍ/rakhoḍ forms under *rakṣāpuṭaka 10555, and three short rak forms under rakṣā 10552. The full primary articles distinguish Gujarati layers-of-ashes/ashes from the nasal Marathi ash-pit formation *rakṣākuṇḍaka 10553. Aspiration, rhotic/retroflex notation, vowels and local transmission remain qualified. Short taːr, bhua, bani, sua and source-gloss issues remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 156: puccha tail extensions', '', 'Saved 37 tail responses: 23 under the canonical *pucchaḍa extension 8249-2 and 14 simple or explicitly documented -la forms under puccha 8249. The full article supplies Nepali puchar, Gujarati puchṛũ, Sindhi puchiṛī, and Awankari puchul. The extension node is cited through CDIAL 8249 prose: the numbered addendum 2 concerns nasal *puñcha and is not the same as the internal extension ID. Pucchin 8250 was compared. Aspiration, rhotics, nasals and vowels remain qualified; mixed responses and unexplained extra elements remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 157: western shep/chep tail series', '', 'Saved 44 tail records under śepyā 12607. Fresh Molesworth pages 800–801 supply śepaṭī/śempaṭī/śempaḍī and explicitly derive śepūṭ from śep, complementing Turner’s ch-/ś- and nasal comparisons. Full responses and URLs are preserved in pass157-molesworth-research.json; no unregistered citation key was invented. Nasal-cluster reduction in cemṭ/semṭ, vowel, aspiration and stop notation are qualified. The seŋt series and other unexplained extensions remain separate; no ancient ṭ suffix is reconstructed.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 158: wind families', '', 'Saved 41 wind records: 27 bayar/byar variants under vātālī 11500, nine bau/bou/vāyū forms under vāyu 11544, and five eastern pobon/pəbən forms under pavana 7978. Vowel contraction, y/h and v/b correspondences are qualified. Turner’s warning that vāyu and vāta can merge remains explicit in the vāyu decisions; these use documented comparanda without claiming universal separation. The longer vāhaḷ series, particles and mixed responses remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 159: ant families and branches', '', 'Saved 57 ant records: seven long pilīl forms under 8201-6, 23 Awankari/Pothwari pel/pil forms under 8201-2, ten pipra/pepura forms under 8201-5, four kamila/kimila forms under 8201, and 13 simple kiḍ/kiḍiyā forms under kīṭa 3193. Turner’s warning about taboo variation, contamination and imperfect grouping is retained. Extra-v kiḍavi and other unexplained suffixes remain separate. The full cimb 4822 article was read but its pinch/tongs prose alone does not establish the remaining ant forms merely because search comparanda were linked there.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 162: mosquito families', '', 'Saved 34 mosquito records under matsara 9757, maśaka 9917 and daṃśa 6110. The full entries distinguish macchar/machrũ, maśā/mah and ḍās mosquito from other insect families. Regional iya endings, r/l variation in machal, sibilants, aspiration and s/h correspondence remain qualified. Compound mosquito-plus-insect responses and other unexplained extensions remain separate. Passes 160–161 remain research-only, with their primary-evidence gaps and rejected fetch documented in passes160-161-research-checkpoint.json.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 163: cloth families', '', 'Saved 25 cloth responses under *lugga 11072-2 and karpaṭa 2871, whose full entries explicitly document lugṛũ/lugḍẽ and kapur/kapaṛu. Vowels, rhotics, retroflex notation and inflection remain qualified, as do the deeper origin disputes and IA borrowing discussed by Turner. Chinna 5047 was read but does not itself establish the remaining cindaro cloth formations; they remain for further research. Mixed cloth phrases were excluded.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 164: bahari broom family', '', 'Saved 33 bahari/bairi/buar broom responses under bahukāra 9188. Full primary prose documents baühārī/bohārī and bahārī/buhārā, with bauārī in the addendum. Contraction, glide realization, aspiration and inflection remain qualified; the more contracted forms, extra-n formations and mixed responses remain for separate work.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 165: vardhani broom forms', '', 'Saved 11 barhan/bherni/boreni broom responses under vardhana 11378, citing its specific sweeping sense 2 and explicit vardhanī/bārni/baṛhnī comparanda. Aspiration, rhotics and vowels are qualified. Turner’s auspicious naming explanations remain attributed hypotheses. Sammārjana 12979a was also read but does not by itself resolve the residual shortened s-/h- broom forms; those remain separate.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 166: mortar families', '', 'Saved 36 records in three families: CDIAL 2360.4 *udukk hala for liquid-retaining ukhal/okhal responses; 3796 khaṇḍana for full and contracted Bhil mortar names explicitly matched by Gujarati; and 12459 śilā for Bhatri sil. Full primary articles and homepage discovery are preserved. IA transmission remains qualified. Short ukhõ/ukhiyo and exceptional ukhyad require additional phonological evidence; extended sila-ut needs its morphology checked. The source mortar gloss is retained for flat grinding-stone terms. No claim is made that every mortar response is resolved.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 167: four roof families', '', 'Saved 30 records under chattvara 4976, *chatti 4971, chadman 4981 and chādana 5017. The last article explicitly includes replacement by a Middle IA causative formation in -āpana; its chauni forms are not asserted to be simple sound-change outcomes of chādana. Aspiration placement in catʰ and initial ś in Bishnupriya are retained as qualifications. The two catʰ / cat responses contain same-stem alternatives; mixed chad/cal and other multi-stem responses remain excluded. Cross-IA transmission remains open where the family is supported.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 168: Bhil paḍāḷ roof', '', 'Saved ten long-ā lateral roof forms under *paṭalāli 7695 following Gujarati paṛāḷ slope of a roof. The printed reconstruction is questioned and remains qualified. Three shorter forms are held between paṭala roof/thatch 7694 and the extended family 7695; paṭala membrane 7693 was checked but not selected. Homepage discovery also found Sheth paḍālī roof, but this is discovery only and was not used to bypass full primary review.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 169: khaprel roofing', '', 'Saved five khaprel/kheprel/khapparɛl roof responses under kharpara 3831, whose full r-suffix branch explicitly includes Punjabi khaprail tile. The tile-to-roof interpretation is marked as metonymy and source glosses remain unchanged. The article warns of variant *kharpa/*khampa stems and multiple suffixes; those limits and uncertain IA transmission are retained.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 170: loft and upper-storey family', '', 'Saved four māḷya/māḷiyā/maḷai/malu roof forms under *māḍa 9996 using Gujarati māḷiyũ loft and the regional lateral family. Roof versus loft is explicitly qualified. Search linked a Marathi loft expression to māla 10088, but the full article means forest/garden; that search edge was not treated as primary evidence. Full koṣṭha 3546 supports rooms and buildings but does not itself supply the selected survey roof sense, so koṭā remains pending further lexical evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 172: garlic l/r branches', '', 'Saved 44 garlic records under laśuna 10990 and the distinct *raśuna subsection 10990-2. Full primary prose supports Prakrit lasuṇa/lasaṇa/lhasuṇa, Assamese lahan, Gujarati lasaṇ and Marathi lasūṇ, alongside the eastern rasun/rasuṇa branch. Vowel and sibilant variation remains qualified. M-final lasim/losom, n-initial forms, thum and mixed garlic/onion responses remain pending. The koṭhā roof search hit checked this turn comes from Shackle, not CDIAL or Platts; its full source evidence remains to be inspected.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 173: pestle and grinding-stone families', '', 'Saved 40 pestle responses under loṭha 11134 and the distinct musala 10223 / *muṣala-or-muśala 10223-2 branches. The full loṭha entry documents both stone rolling-pins and grinding/pounding stones, and survey glosses were preserved. Aspiration and rhotic/retroflex variation remain qualified, including exceptional lorḍʰa notation. S-medial and ś-medial musala forms were assigned to their specific sections; h-medial and liquid-lost forms remain pending rather than selecting between overlapping branches without further evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 174: simple dust family', '', 'Saved 19 simple dust responses under *dhūḍi/dhūli 6835. Full prose and addenda document the selected lateral/rhotic/retroflex family; initial retroflex and aspiration notation remains qualified. Duṛli, dhudur and other extended forms are pending. Full rajas 10581 supplies pollen/dust but does not document the extra suffix in rojaḍu/rodzaḍu, so those were not assigned from search resemblance alone.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 175: mud families', '', 'Saved 18 mud responses under *kicca 3153 and kardama 2869. The former explicitly documents kicaṛ/kicaḍ; the latter documents kādav/kādua and nasal kāṁdau/kā̃daw. Source vowels, affricate notation and dental/retroflex variation are retained as qualifications. Longer kādevəḍu forms require suffix evidence; reordered cik- forms need the cross-referenced *cikka entry, now identified by homepage discovery as 4780 for the next pass. No compound response was reduced to a single stem.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 176: cikka and cikhalla mud', '', 'Saved 14 mud records under cikka 4780 and cikhalla 4784. Lahnda cikkuṛ, Punjabi cikkaṛ and Kotgarhi cikṛɔ support the c-initial rhotic forms; Hindi cihel supports Bagheli cehela/cehila. This distinguishes *kicca 3153 and the three numbered cikhalla families. Same-stem cikaṛ/cikoro alternatives are preserved together. Bagheli cikela remains pending between the article’s cikila comparison and cikkhalla subsection 3; multiword muddy-earth expressions remain unresolved.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 177: door and door-leaf families', '', 'Saved 40 records under kavāṭa 2963 (including kapāṭa and modern kiwāṛ/kebāṛ/kamāṛ) and dvāra 6663 (Gujarati bārṇũ and Kachchhi bāyṇo in the addendum). Conservative kapat transmission and glottal/nasal notation in baʔaṇo/baʔaŋo remain qualified. Vāraṇa 11553 means obstruction and gives a doorstep comparandum; it was not substituted for the door family. Būa and mixed Persian-door responses remain pending.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 178: gate and duvāra', '', 'Saved nine door records under *phāṭṭakka 9069 and *duvāra 6459. Gate versus door shutter and initial retroflex notation remain qualified. Full *jhamp 5338 supplies matting and matted shutters, but does not document the -l- of jhoplu; būā likewise lacks an explicit comparison in the inspected entries. Both groups remain pending.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 179: few and short', '', 'Saved 22 records under the distinct stoka -ḍ- and -l- extension nodes 13720-2 and 13720-3, and ślakṣṇa 12732. Both stoka extensions are unnumbered in the printed article, so citations are CDIAL[13720], not invented numbered subsections. Its Jaunsari and Kotgarhi short sense is explicit. The simple nahna/nānu/nani short forms use a qualified small-to-short comparison; extra suffixes remain pending.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 180: ṭuk short', '', 'Saved 17 simple short forms under *ṭukka 5466-2, whose full primary article explicitly gives Gujarati ṭũk/ṭũkũ small/brief. Nasalization, dental/retroflex and aspiration notation remain qualified. The article calls a deeper truṭ connection very doubtful. Longer -l/-ḍ/-r forms have related piece/twig comparanda but still require evidence for short. Full āskauti 1503 supplies Marathi verbs meaning contract, not the survey adjective ākhuḍa; the latter remains pending rather than deriving the adjective from that verbal entry without evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 181: short stature', '', 'Saved six short-stature forms under *ṭhiṅga 5501 and vāmana 11538. Full articles explicitly supply Punjabi/Hindi/Gujarati ṭhiṅgṇā and regional bauṇā/baunā; medial nasal/aspiration and au-to-o contraction remain qualified. K-medial ṭhīkṇɔ/ṭikkaṇa and aspirated-initial bʰāṇu were not included without further sound evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 182: palm and the nasal-lateral addendum', '', 'Saved 26 palm records under hastatala 14029. Ordinary hath-/het-lateral forms follow the full hathelī comparanda. Four nasal-lateral forms follow the explicit West Pahari hath-naḷe/thənɔḷi addendum, which explains th-t to th-n dissimilation and tāḍa influence; that explanation remains source-attributed. Extra velar hatkela and reordered tarhath compounds remain pending, not silently collapsed into hathelī.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 183: palm compounds and telī', '', 'Saved 12 records as 21 rows: three simple telī-family palms under tala 5731 and nine eastern expressions with ordered hasta 14024 plus tala 5731 components. Bengali genitive hater/hatɛr and interpreted Bishnupriya ator/atol forms retain regional r/l and h-loss qualifications; Oriya toḷohato preserves the reverse component order. No inherited ancient compound node was invented. Full hastaka 14025 gives handles/handfuls rather than a hatkela palm derivation, so that extra velar remains pending.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 184: palm comparisons requiring primary evidence', '', 'This research-only pass saves no assignments. It records explicit holds for garda cushion-to-palm and padma lotus-to-palm comparisons: full CDIAL articles do not supply the proposed hand sense. Existing links were traced to Bundeli and Boehm survey records and were not treated as independent etymological verification. A public Platts query was unavailable and web search produced no relevant dictionary article; no evidence was inferred from that failure. Both simple and compound responses remain intact for later regional research.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 185: simple cīra and cēla cloth', '', 'Saved six cloth records under cīra 4843 and cēla 4910, retaining rhotic/retroflex and lateral/vowel qualifications. Full primary articles explicitly supply clothes in general and regional čilā cloth/dress. Stop-final cida/ciḍā, geminate ciḷːo and extended ciləṛā or cindaro forms remain pending; no generic rag resemblance was used to assign their additional morphology.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 186: unresolved clothing families', '', 'Research-only: 40 records held for audit. Full CDIAL 4023 *gabba documents hole filling rather than clothing; no verified immediate donor emerged from gaba/qaba searches. CDIAL 2547 documents cloaks and upper garments, but the onh-/oneh- group still needs regional evidence for consonant loss and reshaping. These gaps concern lexical identity and sound development, not merely cross-IA borrowing. No assignments were saved. Additional discovery for citth and latta is retained separately for continued review.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 187: dental and retroflex clothing roots', '', 'Research-only: five lʌʈa/laṭːa cloth records held between 10930 *latta and 10917 laṭṭa. Full primary prose gives clothing senses for BOTH: Awankari/Kumaoni lattā clothes and Sindhi laṭo old garment/Bengali-Hindi lāṭ old clothes. The survey retroflex stop cannot be silently normalized to the dental branch. Full 10918 tangled and 10931 foot/kick articles were also inspected; their similar heads do not settle the target clothing etymology. No assignments saved.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 188: spider families', '', 'Saved 21 simple spider records under markaṭa² 9883 and kōlika 3535. Full primary prose supplies regional makara/mākaṛ/mākuro spider forms and Prakrit kōlia plus Marathi koḷī spider; vowel, stop/flap, lateral and ending variation remains qualified, as does local IA transmission. Extra -śa, compounds and karol-type forms are excluded pending their distinct morphology. Homepage discovery also returned f_g2xyrn6mb37ok makaḍaga spider under 9884: full CDIAL 9884 is the grass article, so that existing link was not used as evidence and is flagged for a separate source/graph audit. All 21 new links passed temporary-graph validation and repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 189: yolā spider borrowing family', '', 'Saved nine simple yolā/yolo/yūlā spider records as provisional borrowed links to existing Pashto ǰolā́, OPED entry 17180. The archived full XML explicitly gives weaver and spider and a spider-web example; the neighboring Ushojo survey separately attests yolā for both senses. Direct Pashto versus regional mediation remains unresolved and is stated on every link. The live OPED page could not be opened; the archived primary XML, not that failed request, supports the lexical donor. Full CDIAL jāla, jālakāra and kulāla articles were also inspected. Longer žālāū/žolāū and mixed responses remain pending. No new donor was ingested. Temporary graph validation passed, repeat application changed zero, and shared forms/edges/identity registry were unchanged.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 190: morning families and the paroḍo alternative', '', 'Saved seven morning records: four *savēla forms including two intact savela / savele responses, two consonant-retaining prabhāta forms with learned/local-transmission qualifications, and Malvi paroḍo under prarōha 8747. Both full 8707 and 8747 explicitly prefer prarōha over prabhāta for Gujarati paroḍh dawn; the alternative is retained in the saved evidence. Nasal pārəvā̃t, sakai-type lateral-loss forms and bhinhya/bhinhik remain pending. The full sakāla and vibhāniḥsāra articles were inspected but do not alone settle those local developments. Temporary-graph and preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 191: midday and the longer -niyā formation', '', 'Saved 18 noon records under madhyāhna 9818 and *mādhyāhnaka/mādhyāhnika 10039. Full primary prose distinguishes Prakrit majjhaṇha/majjhaṇṇa from majjhaṇhiya, supporting separate treatment of the simple and longer survey forms. Nasal anticipation, h-loss, vowel and retroflex notation remain qualified, and consonant-retaining forms may reflect learned or regional transmission. Full madhyaṃdina 9805 and madhyānta 9816 were also inspected; the Sindhi mañjhandi ambiguity does not supply a blanket equation for every midday expression. Multiword mathe-dihi and madzon-ḍihi remain pending component analysis. All new rows passed graph, identity and preservation checks, with zero changes on repeat application.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 192: head/above plus day noon expressions', '', 'Saved 14 intact survey expressions as 28 ordered component rows: masta 9926 first, divasa 6333 second. Full articles document head/top/above and the day/sun dīha/dihāḍo/dahāṛɔ families. The noon phrase is interpreted as day/sun overhead, with vowel, aspiration, retroflex and ending differences retained as qualifications. The full divasa article allows ahar crossing and Northwest spread of h-forms; no regular inherited s-to-h rule or new ancient compound is asserted. Source spacing/hyphens remain unchanged. mathedin, madzon-ḍihi, mixed ḍiho/mate-ḍihi and other initial components remain pending. Validation checked both ordered edges for every record and repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 193: half-day noon compounds', '', 'Saved six Maiyã/Ushojo āṛdī/āṛdīs/āṛdez records as 12 ordered component rows: ardha² 644 plus divasa 6333. Full 644 explicitly cites Maiyã aṛ-dī noon and 6333 gives Maiyã dis and Shina dez day. Full ardhadivasa 654 allows a locally formed compound, so no compulsory ancient whole-compound descent is asserted. Vowel length, final s/z and regional transmission remain qualified. The mixed āṛdīs / xārma response remains pending. All ordered edges and source/registry preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 194: unresolved head-time noon expressions', '', 'Research-only: 11 Bhatri/Adivasi Oriya noon records held. Full mūrdhan 10247 explicitly questions the unaspirated muṇḍa head forms as possible muṇḍa 10191 reflexes or crossed formations. Full velā 12115 supplies time meanings but also a separate *veḍā section for Oriya beṛa, requiring branch-level care. No unsupported compound edges were saved. Whole source responses remain in the audit, including standalone munḍ noon.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 195: many forms with competing roots or donor gaps', '', 'Research-only: 28 records held. Full bahutara 9188a and bahura 9193 explicitly leave their shared regional many forms ambiguous; full bhārika 9465 adds a documented much sense. The ziyādā discovery points to an existing Persian parent attributed only to arora; a primary immediate donor/route still needs verification. These family leads remain available, with regional transmission distinguished from missing lexical evidence and unusual morphology. No new assignments saved. Full bhara, bhāra and gañja heap articles were also retained as research evidence.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 196: extended heap-to-many forms', '', 'Research-only: 8 records held with a documented *ḍhēra/*dhēra family lead. Full 5599 distinguishes retroflex heap/many forms from dental Nepali dher/dherai, but does not explain the selected -ka/-khur/-khu additions or altered initials. Full ghana 4424 was also inspected; remaining ghanoso/ghaṇkoj and longer responses require morphology beyond the bare quantity stem. No new assignments saved; primary and homepage discovery evidence is retained for continuation.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 197: joi wife and geminate janni ambiguity', '', 'Saved Dotyali joi wife under yuvati 10504: full primary text explicitly gives Kumaoni joī/jwe woman/wife and Nepali joi wife. Three Dang ǰənni/ǰhənni wife records are held between semantically direct jani 5103 and a possible contraction of jananī 5100, whose article explicitly gives Punjabi jaṇṇī mother. Regional evidence must resolve the gemination and semantic development. Full jāni 5194 is restricted principally to compound-final wife and does not settle this. The saved link passed identity, graph and preservation checks with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 198: Hajong magu wife', '', 'Saved ten Hajong magu wife records under mārga 10071, following the full article’s explicit Bengali māgu/māug woman/wife derivation near the discussion of hair-parting terminology. This unexpected semantic connection is attributed to Turner, not presented as independently demonstrated. Bengali contemptuous register is not imposed on the unqualified Hajong survey gloss. Regional IA borrowing versus inheritance remains unresolved. Full mātr̥grāma 10020 instead gives Sinhala māgama and was inspected separately. All ten links passed graph and preservation checks; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 199: Awankari ran woman/wife', '', 'Saved seven Awankari ran/ṛan woman/wife records under the exact raṇḍa subsection 10593-5. Full primary prose explicitly gives Awankari ran wife and Lahnda/Punjabi rann woman/wife. Retroflex-r notation and local transmission are qualified; widow and derogatory senses from other lects are not imposed on neutral survey responses. Full rājñī 10692 was inspected separately. Validation and source/registry preservation checks passed, with zero changes on repeat application.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 200: bhatār and northwestern husband forms', '', 'Saved ten husband records: four Hajong batar/batʰar under bhartṛ 9402 and six Palula/Torwali/Gawri forms under *bhāriyāpa 9467. Full primary text provides their regional comparanda. Notes preserve aspiration placement, b/v and vowel variation, and Turner’s alternative *bharitṛ/vara comparisons and tentative explanation of Gawri h-. The reconstruction and regional transmission are not presented as settled. Extended bʰʌtaɾʌs, batevu and mixed responses remain pending. Temporary graph, identity and preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 201: man/person terms used for husbands', '', 'Saved 14 man/husband records under manuṣya 9828 and mānuṣa 10049. Full articles explicitly provide regional munus and māṇas/māṇūs forms, plus husband senses and the influence of puruṣa on the munisa branch. Source glosses and nasal/vowel notation remain unchanged. Regional transmission is qualified. Extra -kh/-k, bare muni, manay and mixed/compound responses remain pending. All new rows passed graph, identity and preservation checks; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 202: regional bīnd/vīnd husband family', '', 'Added six Marwari/Dhundari husband records to the existing user-approved regional vīnd family. This is lexical-family membership with unresolved ultimate ancestry, grounded in the exact Mewari vīnd attestation and its preserved original phonemics. b/v and endings remain qualified. The previous audit distinguishes bridegroom from bindu dot; the live dictionary page was unavailable, so no new primary claim is attributed to that request. No new family head or Sanskrit origin was created. Validation and preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 203: regional māṭī husband family and scope contamination', '', 'Saved 17 Rajasthanic/Bhil māṭī/maṭi/mati husband records into the existing user-approved regional family. Mewari dictionary p. 289 col. 2 entry 8 explicitly gives maʈi husband; vowel and dental/retroflex variation remain qualified. Ultimate ancestry remains unknown. Validation caught the curated family head itself in the broad source inventory and rejected a self-link before any write. That node f_fofowqdh5shlk is now held for source-scope reconciliation, not counted as a newly linked survey attestation. The corrected batch passed graph and preservation checks with zero second-application changes. No soil/clay ancestry is asserted.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 204: curated-family scope correction', '', 'Excluded ten curated regional-family heads from survey scope after matching every ID to its original user-authorized family-creation audit. They had inherited survey citation keys and were not independent source attestations. None was newly assigned by this review. This removes one scope hold and nine unresearched non-attestations; all actual survey members and saved links remain. The original inventory and pre-correction scope file are preserved, and pass204-scope-audit.json records exact nodes and provenance. Overlay, compiled files and identity state are unchanged.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 205: small plus sibling expressions', '', 'Saved 50 younger-brother/sister records with 100 ordered component edges: chōṭṭa small plus bhrātṛ brother or bhaginī sister. Full primary articles and addenda support the components; source aspiration, vowel, retroflex and breathy p notation remain qualified, and cross-IA transmission is unresolved. Responses involving bāī, dīdī, unusual extra morphology or discordant agreement were not swept into this batch. All preservation and temporary-graph checks passed; second application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 206: simple younger-brother forms', '', 'Saved seven Maiya/Chiliss žā/ẓā records supported by explicit CDIAL bhrātṛ comparanda and twelve Awank/Gojri pirā/pīrā records matching regional bhirā with initial consonant and vowel notation qualified. Source younger senses remain intact. Mixed responses and piranden were excluded. Both families preserve unresolved regional transmission. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 207: small/young in sibling terms', '', 'Saved seven simple or transparent sibling records with nine ślakṣṇa-family/component links. The full article documents nannā small/young, nāni little girl, nanuā young/child and Gujarati nānũ small. Held 33 extended sibling expressions for local -l/-k/-ḍḍ adjective extensions, kin-term endings or distinct kin stems. Standalone same-survey nānlo/nānlu/nānko small supports segmentation but does not by itself document derivation. Graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 208: interrogative ke', '', 'Held 42 ke/ke̤ what records after comparing full CDIAL ka 2574 and kim 3164. Both belong to the interrogative paradigm, but their listed reflex distributions do not by themselves select the historical inflection behind these particular forms. Local paradigms or primary dictionary analyses are needed. This is an exact-parent question, not solely cross-IA transmission uncertainty.', '', '## Pass 210: simple phalī groundnut', '', 'Saved 30 phalī/phaḷī/phelli groundnut records to the phala family. Full CDIAL explicitly gives Lahnda phallī and Punjabi/Hindi phalī legume pod, with Gujarati phaḷī. Groundnut is the narrower survey sense, not a meaning claimed for Sanskrit or Turner. Same-family slash responses remain intact; regional borrowing routes are qualified. Extended phaleā and phumjya-type responses remain unresearched. All graph/preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Passes 213–214: transparent footpath compounds', '', 'Saved 18 pagdaṇḍī-type records with 36 ordered padga plus daṇḍa component edges; CDIAL explicitly compares the modern compound in 7753 while assigning contracted paiṇḍā-type forms to reconstructed padadaṇḍa. Saved six pai-vaṭ/pag-vaṭ records with 12 foot plus vartman component edges. Full articles support the western regional forms; padga phonology and the vartman/vartis overlap remain qualified. Mixed standalone/compound responses and other extended forms remain outside these batches. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.', '', '## Open discovery: passes 211–212', '', 'Ash bani/vani and garlic thum/thom remain under research; the examined bhasman articles do not directly establish the former and homepage garlic matches lack linked donor evidence. For path ra/rah, Platts supplies Hindustani rah with Persian origin, but the appropriate immediate donor node remains to resolve. A Punjabi ra under CDIAL rajan proved to mean king/title and was excluded from path evidence. Detailed research states are retained in pass211-research-state.json and pass212-research-state.json; these discoveries are not counted as completed reviews.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 215: gati lateral extension in path forms', '', 'Saved six Bagheli/Kaithal geyil/geyl/geli/gelli/gəli/gali path records to existing extension 4009-2. The full primary explicitly gives Hindi gayal/gail/galī and Old Marwari gailo/galo; the printed extension is unnumbered and cited as CDIAL 4009. Homonymous mortar responses remain separate. Vowel and gemination differences and regional transmission remain qualified.', '', '## Pass 216: affricate variants of today', '', 'Saved 21 simple adz/adž/adži/aje/āje/adʒʰ/adʒ(ə) today records to adya 242 after reading adya, adyāpi and adyāhnaḥ in full. Source affricate, aspiration and optional-schwa notation remain intact. Extra -ika/-iku/-ij material remains unresearched.', '', '## Pass 217: red versus red-yam forms', '', 'Saved 28 rakta-family red records, mostly western lateral-extended rātlo/ratalu/rataḷo types plus simple Noiri raṭu. The full rakta entry already includes eastern rātul/rātula red; grouping does not assert an eastern borrowing route or a common reconstructed lateral suffix. The separate raktālu entry means red yam and was excluded. Retroflex/dental notation remains qualified, and nasal-extended raṭno/raṭano remain outside this pass. All three passes preserved unrelated rows, source forms and identity state; temporary graph checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Passes 218 and 220: quantity words', '', 'Saved Gojri baṛa many to vaḍra 11225, whose full addenda explicitly give related much/very usage; the debated deeper origin remains qualified. Held 14 Awank/Gojri muc/mūc or mixed-response records because no primary etymological connection was established. Held three Gojri mato/matā many records because the full matta and mātrā articles do not support that local meaning; held Bhatri bistaṛ many pending discrimination of vistāra and vistara. No English much connection or bedding etymology was inferred. The saved row passed all graph and preservation checks with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 221: further vanara monkey variants', '', 'Saved 25 eastern/western banor/bador/bẽdṛa/banḍar/baṇḍro-type monkey records to vānara 11515. The full article supplies nasal and non-nasal bānar/bā̃dar families and regional syncopated forms; front vowels, nasal notation and dental/retroflex differences remain qualified. The buy/buj family and mixed responses remain separate.', '', '## Pass 222: regional dobu buffalo family', '', 'Saved 24 simple western ḍubi/ḍobi/ḍubo/ḍobu-type buffalo records into the existing user-approved regional family f_fxwwawo3wqdh4. Its original creation audit independently documents Mewari ḍobū and ḍɔbī buffalo; representative head spelling is not a reconstructed ancestor. Ultimate ancestry remains unresolved. Added stop/rhotic extensions were excluded.', '', '## Pass 223: barkara and bokka goat families', '', 'Saved 44 goat records split between barkara 9153 and bokka 9312. The latter explicitly supplies Prakrit bokkaḍa and western bokaḍ forms, supporting the stop-extended Bhil/Bareli group. Held 16 rounded rhotic forms because both articles explicitly overlap through bokrā/bokṛũ and historical crossing. This is a parent-family ambiguity, not merely uncertain IA transmission. All three passes preserve unrelated rows, source data and identity state, with zero second-application graph changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 224: further buffalo families', '', 'Saved 43 buffalo records: western paḍi/pāḍi/paḍo to pāḍḍa 8042.1 and straightforward mahiṣa-family forms to 9964. Full pāḍḍa explicitly includes Prakrit generic buffalo alongside calf/young-buffalo senses; no source gloss was narrowed. The separately numbered pēḍḍa branch remains distinct. Mahiṣa fricative/affricate, vowel and ordinary feminine/oblique forms remain qualified, and same-family slash responses are intact. Ga-prefixed and other unclear extensions remain excluded. Temporary graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.', '', '## Pass 225: pai goat remains unresolved', '', 'Held ten pāī goat records after full paśu 7984 and pāḍḍa 8042 inspection. The former provides Sindhi paha/Kacchi paũ and the latter Pashai pāyaṛék, but neither directly documents a Khowar/Kalasha/Domaaki pai derivation. The missing local phonological/lexical evidence is distinguished from uncertain IA borrowing. No link was saved for this family.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 226: markata monkey versus spider', '', 'Saved 27 simple mākaṛ/makaḍi/makoḍ/makṛi-type monkey records to markata 9882 after inspecting both full monkey and spider entries. Prakrit masculine/feminine and nasal forms, Phalura mākaṛ, Oriya māṅkaṛa and Marathi mākaḍ/makḍī support the selected group. Source vowels, nasal notation and dental/retroflex/rhotic differences remain qualified. No monkey was assigned to spider entry 9883. Longer -iya/-iyu forms and reduced maka/mako forms remain for further review. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 227: Hajong fly plus insect', '', 'Saved ten Hajong masipoka/maśipoka fly records with twenty ordered makṣā plus pōkka component edges. Full articles supply eastern māchi fly and Bengali pokā/Oriya poka insect; source s/ś and vowel notation remain qualified. The modern compound and complete response are preserved, with local transmission unresolved.', '', '## Pass 228: cataka/citaka bird branches', '', 'Saved 22 bird records divided between caṭaka 4571.1 and ciṭaka 4571.2. The full article explicitly gives Nepali caro/cari and eastern carai-type extensions in the first branch, and Hindi ciṛī/ciṛiyā with Bhojpuri ciraī in the second. Source nasalization, affricates, aspiration and vowel notation remain intact. Generic bird glosses were not narrowed to sparrow. Both batches passed graph/preservation checks with zero second-application changes. The parallel ant discovery reconfirmed the earlier global-eleventh audit: full cimb 4822 discusses pinching/tongs, not these ant forms; existing source assignments alone are insufficient and no new ant links were saved.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 229: lice and mosquitoes', '', 'Saved four Danuwar okʷon/okʷan/okʰon louse records to utkuṇa 1728, which explicitly gives Bengali okan and Oriya ukhun/ukhan; saved ten regional pūtī/phūtī/phūthī/phūth mosquito records to phutta 9085, with exact Phalura phutti mosquito and Bshk phit support. Aspiration, labialization and vowel notation remain qualified. Mixed insect responses remain separate. The save passed graph/idempotence and source-preservation checks. A subsequent editorial correction changed Bhatri to Bhateri in ten shared evidence notes, matching language code bhatr; every non-note field and unrelated row remained unchanged, with original save validation and pre-correction overlay preserved in pass229-note-correction.json and its backup.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 230: distinct louse families', '', 'Saved three Kochila Tharu dhil/dhilə records to the specifically numbered ḍhilla branch 5536-6, supported by Maithili ḍhīl louse; saved four Dang Tharu čilur/čɪlra/čɪlər/čilra records to cillaṭa 4828, which explicitly gives Hindi cīlhaṛ/cillar louse. Source phonetics remain intact and local transmission is qualified. Longer Danuwar forms remain pending. Graph and preservation checks passed; second application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 231: fish and lateral fish extensions', '', 'Saved 29 fish records: simple western mas-/maṣ- forms plus directly documented Gawri macoṭa and eastern affricate responses to matsya 9758, and five western lateral forms to the separate existing -l- extension node 9758-3. Full primary prose and addenda document Marathi māsā, Marathi/Konkani māsḷī, Gawri mačoṭá and eastern māch/mās. The unnumbered lateral extension is cited to the base entry rather than inventing a printed subsection. Source vowels, s/ṣ/ś and l/ḷ remain unchanged, with regional transmission qualified. Compounds, shortened forms and unclear subsection choices remain unresearched. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 232: rhotic dog family', '', 'Saved 17 Bhil-area kutəro/kutəru/kutru/kuṭaro/kuṭara/kuṭri dog records to kuttira 3277. Full primary text explicitly provides West Pahari kutar, Old Gujarati kūtiraü and Gujarati/Marathi kutr- forms. Source dental/retroflex spelling and vowels remain unchanged; regional transmission is qualified. Mixed huṇo-plus-kuṭro responses remain excluded. Validation and preservation checks passed, with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 233: cat family and bilauri ambiguity', '', 'Saved two Dang Tharu bilariya cat records to biḍāla 9237.1, supported by the full Maithili bilār/bilāri, Awadhi bilārī and Hindi bilār family with ordinary feminine -iya. Five Dewas/Majhi bilauri records are held: the full article gives long bilār/birlāū comparanda separately from billa-subsection biloṛī child and bilauṭā kitten, leaving the exact au/r formation and branch unresolved. A direct regional dictionary attestation is needed. This is not a hold solely for IA transmission. The two saved links passed graph and preservation checks with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 234: specific cow branches', '', 'Saved 17 cow records: Tharu gāi/gāy-type responses, Phalura gʰāo and Awankari gʰā to gāvī 4147.3, and Kalasha gakʰ to gāvā 4147.2. Full prose explicitly documents their regional comparanda. Survey aspiration, glides, nasalization and final vowels remain unchanged; local ending formation and IA transmission remain qualified. The extra gʌjijã and mixed responses are excluded. Initial calf-family discovery found vatsarūpa 11243, but its full text gives young calf rather than directly explaining the pending vasaḍi cow records. Graph and preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 235: cow terms within the calf family', '', 'Saved Gawri heʦī cow to vatsa 11239: the full article explicitly gives Gawri ēċī cow from vatsikā with NOGaw 27 citation. Initial h and affricate notation remain unchanged. Six western vāsəḍi/vasaḍi/vasaṛi/vasoro cow records are held because full vatsa, vatsatara and vatsarūpa entries offer distinct stems/extensions without a direct local comparison deciding the parent and ending. The adult cow gloss is preserved; the uncertainty is not just transmission. Validation and preservation checks passed, with zero changes on repeat application.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 236: hair and lateral eye forms', '', 'Saved ten Hajong tśul/tśuli hair records to cūḍa 4883.1 and seventeen western ḍuḷu/ḍuḷo/ḍuḷā/ḍula/ḍuḷa/ḍolo eye records to dōla 6582. Full primary prose gives Bengali cul, Assamese suli and Oriya cūḷa hair, plus Prakrit ḍōla and Marathi ḍoḷā eye. Source notation and local inflection remain intact; ultimate history of the hair family and regional transmission are qualified. Reduced eye forms without a lateral remain pending. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero changes on repeat application.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 237: lohita blood variants', '', 'Saved 29 blood records to lohita 11165. Full primary evidence includes Jaunsari loī, Kului lóu, Gujarati lohī and regional lūi/lō. The western n-initial nuhi/noi/nuye/noye group is provisionally linked using the explicit Oriya lohu/nohu and lahu/nahu alternation; no Oriya loan or settled local n-history is asserted. Lateral retroflexion, vowels, nasalization, glides and optional h remain intact. Mixed responses and distinct rakta-type forms remain separate. Graph, identity and preservation checks passed; repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 238: mouth subtypes and missing subsection nodes', '', 'Full tuṇḍa 5853 supports distinct tuṇḍa, tōṇḍa and thōttha mouth branches. Validation discovered that exact nodes 5853-3 and 5853-17 are absent; the first save failed before any overlay write. Saved only two u-vowel tuṇḍ/tũḍə mouth records to the existing base node with subsection 1 citation. Twenty-two supported o-vowel and aspirated forms are held for a section/node repair, with original proposals and pass238-parent-gap.json preserved. No broad-head or remote-donor substitution was made. The corrected save passed graph/preservation checks and repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 239: ragata/rakta blood', '', 'Saved eleven blood records to rakta 10539, supported by the full CDIAL blood sense and Tulpule p.579 entry 2 Old Marathi ragata blood with explicit Sk. rakta etymology. The reciprocal rakta entry at p.578 entry 5 and exact raw source row were inspected. This supplies primary lexical evidence for the epenthetic voiced ragat form. Source vowels, voicing, aspiration, retroflexion and spacing remain unchanged; learned and regional transmission are qualified without asserting an Old Marathi loan throughout the surveys. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 240: bone family and attested extensions', '', 'Saved 45 bone records to haḍḍa 13952, including k-extended forms supported by Kotgarhi hàṛkɔ/Jaunsari hāḍkī/Garhwali hāḍgu, three Palula haḍūŋk records supported by exact haḍḍuṅg, and eastern rhotic forms. Source h loss, voicing, retroflexion, vowels and inflection remain intact; regional transmission remains qualified. Turner’s very doubtful connection with asthi is explicitly not adopted. Graph and preservation checks passed with zero second-application changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 241: skin extensions with medial b', '', 'Saved 25 cambaḍ-/cambṛ-/sambaḍ- skin responses to existing carmaḍa node 4701-2. The full carman entry separately documents the unnumbered -ḍa extension with Gujarati cāmḍī, Marathi cāmḍī and Hindi/Punjabi camṛā. Medial b and local affricate/sibilant, retroflex and vowel differences remain explicitly qualified; no new b morpheme or ancient b-bearing reconstruction is asserted. Two same-family slash responses remain complete. Exact node existence and all graph/preservation checks passed, with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 242: tooth and nasta nose forms', '', 'Saved ten danta tooth records and three nose records to nasta subsection 1. Full primary prose supports regional dā̃t/dand tooth, Torwali natkōl nose and Maiyan nathūr nose. Survey dental/retroflex, aspiration/fricative and vowel notation remains intact. The unexplained extension of the nose forms and regional transmission remain qualified. Non-nasal Dotyali dāḍā, longer tooth responses and nathuna remain separate; a nose-ring comparison alone was not treated as sufficient for the latter. Validation and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 243: Khowar naskar and Nuristani comparisons', '', 'Held six Khowar naskār/neskār/niskār nose records. Bashir 2023 p.102, printed p.89, provides exact naskár but only an unspecified Sanskrit-origin note. PNur nāskār face and Waigali naskār nose and ears/beauty are relevant comparisons; the Waigali karṇa relationship is explicitly an editorial grouping, not established ancestry. Full nāsā and karṇa entries do not determine the whole Khowar analysis. No donor or compound link was invented. Exact local comparison nodes and evidence are preserved in pass243-local-comparanda.json.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 244: remaining simple-gloss ear records', '', 'Reviewed all fourteen remaining records with exact gloss ear. Saved five Kalasha/Bhatri/Dogri forms under karṇa 2830, with reduced segments, aspiration, vowels and punctuation qualified and preserved. Held nine: Bshk æn/en/an need evidence for their initial loss; kamta/kanik/kāiḍā/kanto need ending or compound analysis; Mewari buca has only a crop-eared comparison in bucca 9266; ḍunḍa remains unidentified. The full karṇa and karṇaka entries do not establish the uncertain extensions. All five saved rows passed graph/preservation checks with zero repeat changes. This finishes review of that exact-gloss subset, not all ear-related records or the survey project.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 245: udara belly and missing tonda branches', '', 'Saved seven Gawri var, Mai verī/varī and Marathi ūdar belly records to udara 1932. Full primary text explicitly gives Gawri war and Maiyan wēr; local vowels, v/w, final ī and learned transmission remain qualified. Four Kaithal tōnda-type belly records are held because exact node 5858-2 is absent. Two Kului dʌɳḍə records also require branch choice between dunda/ḍuṇḍa and missing subsection nodes. No mouth parent was substituted. The seven saved links passed graph/preservation checks with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 246: dhiddha belly branches', '', 'Saved sixteen belly records: eleven Awankari/Gojri/Dogri responses under 5589.1, supported by explicit Awankari ḍhiḍ and Punjabi ḍhiḍḍ(h), and five Bshk dār records under the specifically numbered 5589.7 ḍhaḍḍha with Bshk ḍār. Source aspiration, voicing and vowel notation remain unchanged; regional developments are qualified. Eleven related records are held: Turner explicitly leaves Torwali ḍei/Phalura ḍher between branches 4 and 6, and some local e-vowel forms need a branch decision. The similar heap entry 5598 was inspected and not used as a substitute. All saved rows passed graph/preservation checks with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 247: anguli and angudi finger branches', '', 'Saved 69 finger records divided between aṅguli 135 and the canonical aṅgūḍi node 135-2x. Full primary text supplies Awankari/Punjabi and broader Hindi-Gujarati/eastern comparisons in subsection 1, and Gawri/Kalasha/Bshk/Torwali/Kandia comparisons in subsection 2. Source rhotics, laterals, vowels, nasalization, glides, spacing and same-family slash responses remain intact; local reduction and transmission are qualified. Validation first rejected assumed ID 135-2 before any write; exact existing node 135-2x was then resolved and documented in pass247-parent-resolution.json. The corrected save passed graph and preservation checks with zero repeat changes. Finger-breadth entry 134 was inspected and not substituted for finger forms.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 248: regional thehuna and ghunta knees', '', 'Saved 22 knee records: twelve Danuwar/Tharu responses under the existing regional ṭhēhunā family and ten Dotyali/Majhi/Bote forms under ghuṇṭa 4479.2. Full Platts p.366 explicitly gives ṭheʼunā knee, supporting regional knee-family membership without proving the starred reconstruction or adopting its proposed Sanskrit ancestry; the earlier elbow-semantic questions remain separate. Full CDIAL gives Kumaoni ghuno and Nepali ghũṛo knee in the nasal branch. Source phonetics and endings remain intact with local transmission qualified. All graph/preservation checks passed and repeat application changed zero.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 249: tang leg branches', '', 'Saved fifteen leg records under ṭaṅka 5428.1 and ṭaṅga 5428.2, following the full regional comparisons: Lahnda/Punjabi and Kotgarhi in the first, Bengali/Maithili/Bhojpuri and Gujarati in the second. Explicit ṭaṅari and ṭeṅri examples support the corresponding extended responses. Source aspiration, palatalization, vowels, nasalization and optional final g remain intact; transmission and overlap between branches are qualified. Mixed yaŋg/caŋg responses remain separate. All graph/preservation checks passed, with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 250: jangha, padga and latta legs', '', 'Saved seventeen leg records across jaṅghā 5082, padga 7766 and lattā 10931.1. Full primary prose directly documents the leg sense, including Lahnda/West Pahari jaṅgh, Gujarati pāg foot/leg, Marathi pagḍā foot and Punjabi latt leg. Source aspiration, retroflexion, vowels, stress and final inflection remain intact. The broader survey leg gloss was not silently narrowed, and Turner’s unexplained padga sound developments remain qualified. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 251: heart and nail families', '', 'Saved 27 records: nineteen conservative or reduced hṛdaya heart forms, three nasal nakha nail forms and five retained-velar nail responses under nakkha 6914.2. Full primary prose provides the regional heart and nail comparisons; source rhotic loss, vowels, nasalization, aspiration, punctuation and repeated slash response remain intact. Learned transmission and uncertain local developments are qualified. Exact subsection nodes were checked before saving. No longer -ḍo nail forms or unrelated organ words were included. All graph/preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 252: jiva heart forms', '', 'Saved eight jiu̯/dziu/ju/jib/jivaḍo heart responses to jīva 5239. Full primary prose explicitly gives Oriya jī heart and West Pahari jiu mind/heart with jiwa/jiba inflection; Gujarati jivṛo life supplies an extended comparison for Mewari jivaḍo. Local affricates, glides, b/v, vowel reduction and the extension remain qualified, with source spellings unchanged. Longer jiban forms and heartbeat compounds remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 253: black forms and yellow parent ambiguity', '', 'Saved 63 black colour records under kāla 3083, supported by full primary kāro, kāwo, kariyā and kāḷiyũ comparisons. Source gemination, rhotic/lateral variation, reduced vowels and same-family slash responses remain intact; local developments and transmission are qualified. Held 21 yellow records: eleven hildi/holud forms need a decision between haridra colour 13990 and haridrā turmeric 13992, and ten Bishnupriya andi/ondi/ordi forms lack direct evidence for the proposed segment losses. All saved links passed graph and preservation checks with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 254: dhavala white forms', '', 'Saved 28 white records under dhavala 6767, supported by full regional dhaulā, dhaulo, dhorā, dhɔḷũ and dhavaḷ comparisons. Source aspiration, retroflexion, rhotics/laterals, medial v, nasalization, gemination and inflection remain intact. Secondary lateral syllables and -io forms are provisional family assignments with their local history qualified; short dob and other reduced forms remain for separate research. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 255: northern pandara white and krsna black', '', 'Saved 34 colour records under pāṇḍara 8047.1 and kṛṣṇa 3451.1. Full primary text directly names Bshk, Chil, Maiyan and Palula white comparanda, and Kalasha, Bshk, Torwali, Palula and Shina Kohistani black forms. The separate pāṇḍura and proper-name Kṛṣṇa branches were inspected and not conflated. Source vowels, retroflexion, rhotics, sibilants, nasalization and ordinary endings remain intact; exact local developments and cross-IA transmission are qualified. Ushoji kīnono was excluded for further ending analysis. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 256: further white families', '', 'Saved 23 white records across śveta, śvaitra, the unnumbered śukra lateral extension, and ujjvala. Full primary evidence separates Khowar išperu from northern seto/śetto, explicitly supplies sukilo/śuklo, and documents northern and western ujjvala descendants. Exact existing extension node 12506-3 is cited to the unnumbered base article. Source phonetics remain unchanged; transmission is qualified. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 257: bhura white and citta ambiguity', '', 'Saved three bhuro/bura white records under bhrūra 9690 and one Jaunsari ciṭu under śvitra 12772, supported by the full entries’ explicit regional white senses. Held three dental/mixed citta responses because both citra and śvitra provide relevant comparisons, and six Pauri Bareli bullo responses because the Maithili bhulla light-brown comparison does not establish their local extension and semantics. Source forms and glosses remain intact. All saved rows passed graph and preservation checks with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 258: northern red families', '', 'Saved sixteen red records under kraviya 3585.2, lohita 11165 and lohila 11168. Full primary text directly gives Khowar krui, Bshk lōu, Palula lohílu and Shina Kohistani līlṷ. Alternative lohuta analysis and documented nīla crossing remain qualified; source phonetics and same-family Khowar slash responses are unchanged. Held two Torwali records because their appended lohuṭa derivation does not establish lohila ancestry, and one mixed Palula lo/lohīḷū response because its short variant is unresolved. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 259: eastern ranga red', '', 'Saved fifteen eastern red records under raṅga 10560. Full primary prose gives Bengali rāṅā and Oriya raṅgā red, including a comparison for nasal forms without the following stop. Local retroflexion, vowels and gemination remain qualified; cross-IA transmission is unresolved. The unrelated defective/wretch raṅga subsection was excluded. A separate discovery note records the unresolved immediate-donor issue for remaining lāl forms, which were not assigned in this pass. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 260: leaf-colour compound and pattra leaves', '', 'Saved forty records with forty-four assignments: four Hajong pataroŋ green responses receive ordered pattra + raṅga component links, and thirty-six leaf responses receive pattra links. Hajong pata leaf is independently documented in the same survey at p.42; full primary entries support the proposed modern leaf-colour analysis, whose exact local history remains qualified. The leaf batch uses documented Punjabi/Awankari, Bshk, Gawri, Kohistani and Kotgarhi comparanda. Source phonetics and complete same-family slash responses remain intact. Nasal pānṭ- and other competing families remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 261: parna leaf variants', '', 'Saved forty-one leaf records under parṇa 7918.1, supported by full simple pān/pānu comparisons, Gujarati pā̃dṛũ leaf and explicitly reduced Kalasha forms. Western nasal-plus-stop forms are provisional regional-family assignments; their exact ṭ/ḍ/ṭh extensions, nasal place and local history remain qualified. Source forms and ordinary-leaf glosses remain unchanged, with no conflation of the separate betel-leaf discussion. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 262: pallava leaves', '', 'Saved five western pālo/pālu/polā leaf records under pallava 7969. Full primary prose supplies explicit leaf and foliage senses, including Gujarati pālav/pāl and Marathi pālā/pālẽ. Source vowels and singular leaf glosses remain unchanged; regional transmission and local developments are qualified. The foliage head is distinguished from the cloth-strip homonym. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 263: tree families', '', 'Saved fifty-eight tree records across gakṣa 3949, jhāṭa 5362.1, būṭṭa 9297.2 and bōṭṭa 9297.3. Full primary entries give direct regional tree senses. The northern u/o branches are kept distinct; source aspiration, affricates/sibilants, retroflexion, vowels and ordinary endings remain intact with local developments and transmission qualified. Longer k-extended jhāḍ forms, mixed unrelated responses and uncertain aspirated būṭ forms remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 264: mula and jata roots', '', 'Saved twenty-six root records under mūla 10250.1 and jaṭā 5086.1. Full primary entries explicitly document lateral and reduced root forms, and regional jaṛ/jari/jauṛ roots. Source vowels, lateral/rhotic quality, glides, gemination and ordinary endings remain intact; exact local reductions and transmission are qualified. Khowar mūḍa, mūrdhan and longer compound responses are not conflated with these assignments. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 265: eastern cer roots', '', 'Held the remaining twelve eastern cer/ceṛ root records after inspecting exact Oriya, Bhatri, Halbi, Bonda and Didayi comparanda. Their local records establish the forms but not a parent or borrowing direction; an unexplained Bonda De. annotation was not expanded by guesswork. Similar Shina cheríṣ remains unlinked, and an empty PBr comparison head was not treated as an etymon. Evidence is preserved in pass265-local-comparanda.json. This research pass adds no overlay rows.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 266: peda and birwa trees', '', 'Saved ten tree records under source-backed pēḍa addendum 8377a and vīrudha 12060. Full primary prose explicitly gives peṛ tree and regional birwā plant; the Bagheli tree sense is retained with semantic breadth qualified. There is a separate curated pēḍa node f_rr7dv53h3a5pm, flagged as a duplicate candidate without merging either node. Source phonetics remain intact. Northern nā/neṛ root searches did not yet resolve their parents; those records remain unresearched. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 267: phulla flowers and phala fruit', '', 'Saved forty-five flower/fruit records under phulla 9092 and phala 9051 respectively. Full primary evidence includes regional fricative forms, Kotgarhi phulṛu flower and reduced Kumaoni phaw fruit. Source aspiration, lateral quality, vowels, final inflection and the distinction between fruit and flower remain intact; local extensions and reductions are qualified. Nasal flower forms, unexplained k-initial forms and longer compounds remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 268: distinct northern flower families', '', 'Saved twenty-two flower records under puṣpa 8303.1, puṣya 8306 and sphuṇṭati 13846. Full primary text directly names the Kalasha, Palula, Gawri and Torwali sibilant forms and Bshk/Chil nasal flower forms. Learned transmission, alternative ultimate derivations and local phonetics remain qualified. Source spellings and the complete same-family slash response remain intact. Longer Ushoji pūśūrū and other unresolved extensions remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 269: Hadauti pasab flower', '', 'Saved five Hadauti pasab flower records to prasava 8827 with explicit supporting cross-reference citation to 8848, which documents prasava flower in the Mahābhārata. Final vowel loss and b/v correspondence remain qualified, and the broader sū/śvi discussion is not treated as settled. Two pasam/pasāmb records are held for their unexplained nasal ending. Puṣpa and prasūna were inspected as comparisons. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 270: collective fruit expressions', '', 'Saved one Kochila pəlepʰul fruit response with two ordered phala/phulla component links, supported by the same survey’s independent phal phul response and full primary component evidence. The internal e remains qualified without an invented affix. Held Kathoriya phalphɔl because fruit repetition and fruit-plus-flower remain competing analyses, and Dang phəl phulwar for its unexplained ending. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 271: seed forms and compound uncertainty', '', 'Saved three Majhi/Bote bʸu seed records under bīja 9250, supported by explicit Nepali biu and regional glide forms. Held five Danuwar bicahan/bicahān/biha records because the distinct bījadhāna and bījadhānya entries do not establish their exact local compound and consonant history. Source forms remain intact; transmission is qualified. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 272: thorn branches', '', 'Saved eighteen thorn records under kaṇṭaka 2668.2, kaṇṭa 2668.1 and śūla 12575. Full primary text supplies direct regional thorn senses. Source aspiration, uvular notation, vowels, nasalization and complete same-family slash responses remain intact; exact local development and transmission are qualified. Three short Bshk thorn responses are held for branch and reduction evidence. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 273: Khowar and Kalasha thorn leads', '', 'Held seven thorn records: five Khowar zux/zox/dzux responses have exact Bashir 2023 p.52 (printed p.39) dzox/zox support and a tentative Wakhi-origin note, but no identified donor form. Two Kalasha cũk responses remain unexplained; full cukra describes sour plants and śūka awns/bristles without establishing these local forms. Exact dictionary comparison records are preserved in pass273-local-comparanda.json. No new overlay rows were added.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 274: milk families', '', 'Saved thirty-six milk records under dugdha 6391, gorasa 4312 and kṣīra 3696, using full direct regional milk comparisons. Source dental/retroflex and affricate notation, vowels, rhotics and same-family slash responses remain intact; local development and transmission are qualified. One Bshk śīr response is held pending evidence separating local affricate development from a possible Iranian loan. Longer milk compounds remain separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 275: rice families', '', 'Saved fifty rice records under cāmala 4749, taṇḍula 5637 and bhakta 9331. Full primary entries supply direct eastern cāur/cāl, northern talun/ṭalåⁿ and regional bhāt comparisons. Original rice/uncooked-rice/cooked-rice glosses, spacing, vowels, affricates, rhotics, laterals and nasalization remain intact. Exact local reductions and Khandesi medial v remain qualified; mixed alū responses stay separate. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines += ['', '## Pass 276: coksa and sali rice', '', 'Saved fifteen western rice records under cōkṣa 4918 and śāli 12415. The full cōkṣa article explicitly gives Gujarati cokhā and Kachhi caukhā rice, supporting the semantic link beyond clean. Gujarati/Marathi sāḷ supplies the other family; Noiri hal is provisional with local s/h history qualified. Source spacing, aspiration, vowels and lateral quality remain intact, and broad rice glosses were not narrowed to a preparation state. All graph and preservation checks passed with zero repeat changes.']
lines.append('\n## Pass 277: kudri cereal mismatch\n\nFive western kudri/kudu.ri/kuḍiri rice records are held against CDIAL 3515 kōdrava: Gujarati kodrī is a close formal match, but the full article identifies millet. Regional extension or elicitation identification requires checking. No assignments were saved in this pass.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 278: sixty-day rice name\n\nOne Jaunsari saṭi rice record links provisionally to ṣaṣṭikā 12806 on full regional rice comparanda. The source unaspirated stop and broad gloss remain intact; sixty-day ripening is not asserted as a survey observation. Competing prasātikā and vrīhi articles were checked without extending them to unsupported remaining rice forms.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 279: meat family\n\n57 eastern and western meat records link to māṃsa 9982, checked against the full article and addenda. Eastern nasal and diphthong forms have direct regional comparanda; western mah/maha and māx are provisional weakened-sibilant family matches. Short mā/mān and metathesized maśaŋ remain unassigned for further checking.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 280: salt and oil\n\n30 records link to lavaṇa 10978, mṛṣṭa 10299, taila 5958 and cikkaṇa 4782 on full dictionary prose. Both slash alternatives were checked for shared family membership. The older parsed mīṭha salt record f_rtca4ywenszao points to 10139 miṣṭa mixed, whose full prose does not support salt; flagged without modifying that unrelated record. Compound salt modifiers, Bishnupriya thou̯ oil and western ten-type oil remain for research.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 281\n\n17 fish records link to exact matsya, matsiya and l-extension branches. Full prose and addenda support Kullu and regional liquid forms. Unexplained northern cimb forms and longer responses remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 282\n\n37 wheat/barley records link to godhūma 4287, yava 10431 and kaṇika 2665. Full regional comparanda support reduced gyu/gheu and kankh. Forms with unexplained additions, cereal mismatches and long alternatives remain unassigned.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 283\n\nSeven cereal records held: yov wheat, bājro/kodro barley have unsupported crop-gloss differences; yūār/yoār barley have unexplained final r. The full millet articles do not validate the existing parsed bājrā barley comparison f_bh5kjlsauqd6c. Original records remain intact; no assignments in this pass.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 284\n\n49 egg records link to āṇḍa 1111 and ḍimba 5550. Full prose supports northern reductions, Kalasha and Lahnda/Awankari r-extensions and western front-vowel forms. Longer responses, l-extensions and extra-initial Kullu forms remain for research; deeper competing āṇḍa origin proposals are not resolved.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 285\n\n21 fat records link to vasā 11443 and medas 10323, supported by full regional comparanda and addenda. Nine mīny/mī̃y/mī̃nź records are held between majjan and medya histories; the primary articles explicitly describe irregular changes and crossing. Bhateri is kept distinct from Bhatri. Basīl extensions and other fat terms remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 286\n\n15 water records link to udaka 1921.1, nīra 7552 and pānīya 8082. Full northern comparanda support ūkh and wī-type forms; Palula bī is a qualified strengthened-labial match. Source glottalization, aspiration and vowels are preserved. Longer responses and par remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 287\n\n48 smoke records link to dhūma 6849.1 and dhūmikā 6849.2 after checking five full primary articles. Gujarati dhumāṛo supports regional retroflex-extended forms with local history qualified. Bshk dīmī glottalized forms use the explicit second section. Less clear k/ṅ forms, lateral extensions and h-final forms remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 288\n\nSix ash records link to bhasman 9425.1 and chādi 5020, checked against full prose. Ten reduced kh-forms are held: kṣāra versus possible regional khāk comparison is not resolved by local evidence. The full kṣāma nasal Assamese comparison does not establish the nonnasal chādi responses. Broader bhua and svāgo groups remain for research.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 289\n\nNine t-initial dust records link provisionally to dhūḍi/dhūli 6835. Full prose explicitly discusses dh~t variation and possible tuṣa influence; this is not a newly asserted regular sound law. Longer raj- and duṛli-type forms remain unresearched after full rajas and dhūḍi comparison.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 290\n\n92 cloud/rain records link to megha 10302 and vārdala 11567. Both meanings are explicitly documented in the full primary articles. Conservative velars, northern reductions and western retroflex/lateral forms retain phonetic and cross-IA transmission qualifications. Mixed-family alternatives and longer expressions remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 291\n\n28 rain records link to varṣati, varṣa section 1, vṛṣṭi and varṣārātri. Khowar boṣik remains explicitly verbal despite the survey rain gloss. The published vārṣa alternative for northern nouns and varṣartu alternative/crossing for barsāt-type forms remain in every affected evidence note; these are provisional conventional family links, not resolved exclusive ancestries. Multiword rain responses remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 292\n\n17 wind records link to exact vātala/vātara branches, vāta and vāyu using full regional comparanda. Published vāta-vāyu convergence and the Hindi bāl alternative remain explicit in evidence notes. Western vahəḷo and northern ūśi groups remain for research.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 293\n\nNine leheṛa/lehra/lehera wind records are held against lahari 10999: the full wave entry does not establish the regional wind/gust meaning, and the retroflex branch must also be distinguished. No assignments in this research pass.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 294\n\n19 lightning records link to vidyut 11742 and vidyullatā 11745 on full regional comparanda. Short affricate forms and longer lateral forms remain separate; Palula medial weakening and Bhil initial reduction are qualified. Longer suffixes and multiword expressions remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 295\n\n15 lightning responses built from flash/shine link to cammakka 4676.2 and the existing lasakkati extension 10993-2. Full prose supports the regional flash and verbal forms, with ordinary endings and transmission qualified. Unnumbered lasati extension cites the base article. Mixed barex responses and longer lightning phrases remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 296\n\n93 day/night records link to divasa, dina and rātrī on full regional prose and addenda. Dihāṛā/dahāṛo and shorter Pahari day forms retain the published ahar-crossing and transmission qualifications. Only same-family slash alternatives were saved. Night affrication follows the explicit Kotgarhi/Koci rāc comparison; compounds and opaque short forms remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 297\n\n30 evening records link to saṃdhyā 12918 and vikāla 11625. Full conservative and reduced evening comparanda support the selected eastern and Pahari forms. The bihar/behera liquid history remains provisional, following the explicitly uncertain Hindi bīaṛ comparison. Longer phrases and opaque reductions remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 298\n\n11 morning records link to traṭ 5988 and sakāla 13067. The crack-to-dawn meaning is explicitly documented for Hindi and Old Marwari. The traṭ/taṭ alternative and western sakāla liquid changes remain qualified. No unsupported prātar head was substituted for the unresolved praṭeh response.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 299\n\n39 sun/moon records link to candra 4661.2 and sūrya 13574.2 with full regional evidence. The distinct sūriya/sūrī branches are preserved. Broader existing sūraj links were not copied without resolving their exact primary basis, and candramas-like longer moon forms remain unresearched.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 300\n\nSaved 12 star records: five Bshk/Tor forms to tara section 1, three Bhil taru forms to taraka section 4, three candani-family forms to candrana with explicit Marathi star semantics, and conservative Odia naksatra. Source spellings and qualified local/contact histories preserved. Longer caŋḍuli and ambiguous eastern tariya branches remain for research.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 301\n\nResearch-only: held NDu sukūvā and sūkalā star after reading full śukra 12506. Pali sukka has planet/star semantics, but the local morphology and śukra versus śukla/l-extension choice remain unsupported. Do not silently narrow the survey gloss to Venus.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 302\n\nSaved 25 sky/cloud records to gagana 3950, ambara section 1, and akasa 1008. Full primary comparanda directly support Jaunsari gen/goyan and northern agha/aga cloud or sky. Preserve Turner’s explicit uncertainty about Dardic k retained as g, and qualify regional fricative or initial-vowel loss. No shared compiled data changed.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 303\n\nSaved 42 sarag/harag sky records to svarga 13910. Previously missing regional evidence was found in cached Platts p.655: sarg/sarag/surag explicitly from Prakrit sarago and Sanskrit svarga, with sky semantics. Mewari sarag/harag and Halbi sarag provide regional comparanda. Final nasal forms remain unresearched; local devoicing, aspiration and s/h variation qualified.\n')
lines.append('\n## Pass 304\n\nSaved 12 cloud/sky records: northern azo/azu and eastern ap or Mewari ab to abhra 549 section 1, and Gawri albena to exact abhriya subsection 549-2. Full primary entry explicitly supports the regional forms and gives the Gawri secondary-suffix analysis. Retain qualified local devoicing/aspiration loss and transmission. Meghala and other unexplained cloud extensions remain for research.\n')
